 ],
 "literal": "Even in the festival where warriors gathered / Even in the tuṇaṅkai dance where women embraced / I do not see anywhere the worthy lord / I too am a dancing-arena woman, my hand's / sliced conch shining bangles made loose / the majestic lord too is a dancing-arena man.",
 "translation": "Neither in the festival where warriors gather, nor in the tuṇaṅkai dance where women hold hands and dance, do I see my worthy lord anywhere. I too am a dancer on the stage; and the majestic lord, who made the shining bangles of sliced conch slip from my hands, is also a dancer on the stage.",
 "modern": "வீரர்கள் கூடிய திருவிழாவிலும் நான் அவரைப் பார்க்கவில்லை; பெண்கள் கைகோர்த்து ஆடும் துணங்கைக் கூத்திலும் நான் அவரைப் பார்க்கவில்லை. தகுதியுடைய என் தலைவனை எங்குமே காணவில்லை. நானும் ஒரு கூத்தாடும் பெண் தான்; என் கைகளில் அணிந்திருந்த, சங்கு அறுத்துச் செய்த விளங்கும் வளையல்களைக் கழலச் செய்த பெருமை பொருந்திய என் தலைவனும் ஒரு கூத்தாடும் ஆண் தான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine who is searching for her lover in public gatherings. She looks for him in the festive assembly of warriors and in the lively tuṇaṅkai dance performed by women, but she cannot find him anywhere. She describes herself as a female dancer on the stage, and her majestic lover as a male dancer. The grief of separation from him has caused her body to thin, making her shining conch-shell bangles slip off her wrists. By identifying both of them as performers on a stage, she highlights the dramatic and public nature of her search and their shared bond. The key words are 'māṇtak kōṉ' (worthy lord), 'tuṇaṅkai' (an ancient group dance), and 'āṭukaḷamakaḷ' (dancing woman).",
